WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.192 --> 00:00:02.775
(smooth music)

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:05.513
- What's up, guys?

00:00:05.513 --> 00:00:07.300
Michael here to talk about a movie

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:10.270
that made me wish I got to
moonlight as a fluffy animal

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:12.550
during middle school, "Turning Red."

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:14.440
Pixar's newest entry explores all

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.070
of the pressure and horrors

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:17.870
of becoming a young
adult through the story

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:20.810
of a girl who turns into a giant red panda

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:23.140
whenever she gets too emotional.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:24.550
Oddly relatable stuff.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:25.860
And while I'm pretty sure none

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:27.610
of you have literally transformed

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:29.830
into an ancestral panda during puberty,

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:32.630
we've probably all had the
experience of being an awkward

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:35.460
and confused 13-year-old
with a changing body

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.180
and raging hormones.

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:39.780
But is this movie about
more than just that?

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.560
Is this exploration of the dark side

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:42.770
of emotional repression

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:45.070
also a window into the broader world

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:46.880
of psychosexual development?

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:50.590
Let's find out in this Wisecrack
edition on "Turning Red,"

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:52.440
Unleashing Your Panda,

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:53.400
and spoilers ahead
(alarm blaring)

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:54.233
for "Turning Red,"

00:00:54.233 --> 00:00:56.850
which you can currently
watch right now on Disney+,

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:58.580
who isn't sponsoring this video,

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:01.020
but hey, they wanna pay, we're down.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.380
So what can an animated film about a girl

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:05.240
who turns into a panda

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:08.520
tell us about the psychology
behind emotional repression?

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:09.353
We'll get there.

00:01:09.353 --> 00:01:11.377
But first, let's meet our main character.

00:01:11.377 --> 00:01:14.320
"Turning Red"'s about 13-year-old Mei Lee,

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:15.980
nicknamed Mei Mei.

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:17.260
- I've been doing my own thing,

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:20.910
making my own moves, 24/7/365.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:22.540
- [Michael] Clearly Mei's crushing it.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:24.230
- All about that hustle. Am I right?

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:25.800
- She has a crew of best friends,

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.156
excels scholastically, can
play a rippin' flute solo,

00:01:29.156 --> 00:01:30.090
(fast-paced flute music)

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.130
and seems to genuinely enjoy helping

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:34.060
her mom manage the family temple,

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.390
and in a key sign of early 2000s life,

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.690
she's also deeply emotionally
invested in a boy band.

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:42.570
Much of what's happening in
Mei's life fits pretty well

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:43.940
with Sigmund Freud's description

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:46.400
of the psychosexual
development of children,

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:47.650
which is basically the process

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:52.400
where we go from disinterested
in sex to horned up 24/7.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.540
According to this theory,

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.520
around the ages of seven through 12,

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:57.810
children begin to assert
their own independence,

00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:59.930
and move beyond their childhood ego

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:02.410
as they learn to love
and care about others,

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:05.370
and Mei seem to be on the
precipice of all of this.

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:08.330
In one early scene, her friends
urge her to assert herself,

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:11.510
cancel plans with her
mom, and come do karaoke.

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:13.750
Though Mei seems tempted, she resists,

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:16.509
not wanting to let her
mom down on cleaning day.

00:02:16.509 --> 00:02:19.910
- I can't. It's cleaning day.

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:22.840
- Mei, every day is cleaning day.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:23.673
- And for the most part,

00:02:23.673 --> 00:02:26.880
everything seems pretty
chill, until that night

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.040
when Mei's casual doodling turns

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:32.920
into a pretty erotic picture
of corner store hunk, Devon.

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:36.330
It's clear that Mei's expressing
a type of unconscious lust

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:40.390
or desire, which, based on her
reaction, seems new for her.

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:42.700
This suggests she might be
entering the final phase

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:46.230
of psychosexual development
in Freud, the genital stage,

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.450
which typically happens around 13.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.220
And this leads to a type of anxiety

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:52.590
or shame that sends Mei under her bed,

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:56.210
seemingly hiding from the
watchful eyes of, say, God,

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:57.890
or, you know, her mom.

00:02:57.890 --> 00:02:59.090
If you grew up religious,

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:01.223
and ended up accidentally
inventing masturbation

00:03:01.223 --> 00:03:03.870
when you were 13, then
you know what we mean.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:04.730
And this right here

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:07.650
is the film's first clear
example of repression.

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:09.870
Mei tries to hide the
drawings from her mother,

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:11.860
seemingly fearing that she'll be judged

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:13.340
for her fledgling desire.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.810
This, of course, is exactly what happens.

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:17.820
Mei's mom can pounce her daughter's shame

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:19.960
and anxiety by showing the drawings

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:22.600
to Devon and a bunch of Mei's classmates.

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:23.700
The subsequent teasing

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.910
only causes Mei more anxiety and shame.

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.320
Freud can help us better
understand what's happening here,

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:32.510
particularly in regards to
Mei's initial repression

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:33.500
of her desires.

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:35.240
Now Freud first noticed repression

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:36.330
when his patients struggled

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:38.820
to recall past memories during analysis.

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:40.420
He concluded that this had to do

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.210
with an unconscious mechanism

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:43.640
by which our human minds

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:45.200
push down and hide,
(people shouting)

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.460
i.e. repress, shameful memories,

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:50.220
so we don't have to
actively deal with them.

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:51.780
That's probably why, for the life of me,

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:53.690
I can barely remember
anything that happened

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:55.577
between the ages of 11 and 15.

00:03:55.577 --> 00:03:57.330
And I'm just gonna trust my unconscious,

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:59.100
and assume it's for good reasons.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.130
This led old Sigmund to develop a theory

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:03.350
of repression that involves two stages.

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:05.490
First, there's primal repression,

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:06.810
in which a child realizes

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:09.790
that acting on certain
desires leads to anxiety

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:11.620
and other unpleasant emotions.

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:14.470
The child subsequently
represses those desires.

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:17.340
This eventually blossoms
into repression proper,

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:19.550
in which repression
leads to the development

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:20.820
of the superego.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:23.710
That's a sort of
self-regulated internal law

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:27.000
or conscience often modeled on
a parent or religious figure.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.050
This superego then bosses around our ego,

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:32.270
which is the part of the
mind we'd usually associate

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:33.840
with our identity,

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.000
and helps keep our innate
impulses and desires at bay.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:39.800
These desires are what Freud calls our id,

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:43.250
which is basically the Four
Loko-chugging frat boy part

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:44.390
of your personality.

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:46.580
In this case, Mei's id is expressing

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:49.210
a sexual desire for Devon,

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:51.470
but then this desire results in shame

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:53.200
and other negative consequences.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:56.690
So her superego starts to
create a moral structure,

00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:57.670
under which this type

00:04:57.670 --> 00:05:01.730
of desire and behavior is
bad and must be repressed.

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:03.657
- This will never happen again.

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:06.620
- And her poor ego now has

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:09.200
to try to balance these
two opposing forces.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.380
Of course, repression isn't
necessarily a bad thing,

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:15.280
like if you have a strong
unconscious desire to strip

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:17.460
down to your underwear, jump
up on a conference table

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:19.600
at your next work meeting
while flinging lunch meat

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:20.550
at your colleagues,

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:23.277
it's a good thing you
have a superego to say,

00:05:23.277 --> 00:05:25.960
"No, bro, that's weird. Don't do that."

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:27.340
However, it's not good

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:30.120
when a normal and healthy
desire gets repressed.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:33.130
In fact, that can come with
some serious side effects.

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:35.270
It's like the unconscious
version of what happens

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:37.500
when the adults in the
Footloose town forbid

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:38.770
the teens from dancing.

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:40.560
If you tell the kids they can't dance,

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:42.856
they're just gonna wanna dance more.

00:05:42.856 --> 00:05:44.805
♪ Hot ♪

00:05:44.805 --> 00:05:46.850
♪ Loaded like a gun ♪

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:48.720
- [Michael] And Mei
realizes the side effects

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:50.850
of her repression pretty quickly,

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:52.690
when she wakes up the next morning

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:54.360
as a giant red panda.

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:57.900
This transformation literalizes
some of her fears and shame,

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:01.580
namely that having normal
teenage desires is turning her

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:02.700
into a monster.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:05.230
And, of course, the movie
isn't not using this

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:07.950
as an allegory for a young
woman getting her first period,

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:11.390
as Mei's mother's initial
reaction is to get her ibuprofen

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:13.640
and a shocking variety of pads.

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:17.040
But the film uses the
specificity of Mei's experience

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:20.440
of transitioning into womanhood
to make a much broader point

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:22.010
about the role of repression.

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:23.090
We learn early on

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:25.880
that the panda is brought
out by strong emotions,

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:27.390
and it's not just negative emotions.

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:30.960
Even a box full of adorable
Pixar kittens threatens

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:32.360
to unleash the beast.

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:34.210
But this paints a clear message.

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:37.050
Strong emotions of any kind are bad,

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:40.510
and should be kept inside where
they can't cause any harm.

00:06:40.510 --> 00:06:44.070
Now Mei learns that one of her
ancestors asked to be turned

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:46.300
into a red panda to defend her family

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:48.120
while the men were away at war.

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:49.630
Ever since, each woman

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:52.960
in her lineage has developed
a panda of their own.

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:54.240
However, they've all gone

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:57.380
through a religious ceremony
to separate from their panda,

00:06:57.380 --> 00:07:00.940
trapping its essence in a piece
of jewelry or other token.

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:03.750
If Mei can just make it
to the next red moon,

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:06.580
she can exercise that
furry critter for good,

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:09.640
but in the meantime, being
forced to contain her panda

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:12.840
just furthers Mei's established
pattern of repression.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:15.710
Her experience of sexual
desire led to shame and guilt,

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:17.540
which caused the panda to emerge,

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:19.300
and now keep the panda at bay,

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:22.440
she needs to repress any
further desires and emotions,

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:25.690
even positive ones, so that
she can remain a model student

00:07:25.690 --> 00:07:28.890
without revealing her
very furry family secret.

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:31.570
Now, we already talked about the superego,

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:34.430
that part of the unconscious
that sets up all types of rules

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:38.400
and regulations so that our
scumbag id can't run rampant,

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:41.120
and Mei, with the help of
her family's mythology,

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:42.960
takes on a new superego structure

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:45.910
specifically designed to repress the hell

00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:48.030
out of that adorable panda.

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:48.920
- Happy?

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:50.970
- Oh yeah, that's the stuff.

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:52.780
- [Michael] But even with
this family narrative,

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:55.720
Mei slowly embraces her
emotions and passions,

00:07:55.720 --> 00:07:58.740
and starts turning panda
whenever she feels like it.

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:00.700
Though she's supposed
to keep her panda at bay

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:03.580
until the ritual, she
realizes being a panda is,

00:08:03.580 --> 00:08:06.230
as you might expect, super fun,

00:08:06.230 --> 00:08:08.370
not to mention quite
financially lucrative,

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:10.030
and her best friends encourage it,

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:12.380
noting that it's bringing
out a whole new side

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:14.410
of Mei that they really love.

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:15.490
- You really changed.

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:18.380
And I'm proud of you.

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:20.900
Just don't get rid of all of it.

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:22.192
- However, in doing this,

00:08:22.192 --> 00:08:24.560
Mei's once Lorelai and Rory relationship

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:27.140
with her mother begins to slowly fracture.

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:27.973
And to understand

00:08:27.973 --> 00:08:29.960
this fracturing
mother-daughter relationship,

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:31.700
we need to kiss Freud goodbye,

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:32.930
as he was notorious

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:36.510
for framing psychological
development around male sexuality,

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:39.100
and that's led many
subsequent theorists to worry

00:08:39.100 --> 00:08:40.700
that thinkers like Freud failed

00:08:40.700 --> 00:08:42.810
to adequately understand the particulars

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:44.340
of female sexual development.

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:45.290
This is exactly

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:48.210
what Bulgarian French theorist
Julia Kristeva thought.

00:08:48.210 --> 00:08:51.330
Her own account of
psychosexual development lands

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:54.770
with the symbolic stage, i.e.
when children enter the world

00:08:54.770 --> 00:08:57.310
of linguistic communications,
social relations,

00:08:57.310 --> 00:08:58.430
the law, et cetera.

00:08:58.430 --> 00:09:00.340
During this phase, a
child develops a sense

00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:02.790
of identity that is
separate from the mother,

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:05.880
and this process involves
something called abjection.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:08.900
Abjection basically happens
when we encounter a rupture,

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:12.730
specifically, the distinction
between ourself and the other.

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:15.630
This causes a breakdown
in our sense of meaning.

00:09:15.630 --> 00:09:18.570
According to Kristeva,
"The abject confronts us

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:21.130
with our earliest attempts
to release the hold

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:24.920
of maternal entity even before
existing outside of her,

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:27.130
thanks to the autonomy of language.

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:29.640
It is a violent, clumsy breaking away

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:31.640
with the constant risk of falling back

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:35.310
under the sway of a power as
securing as it is stifling."

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:37.876
Surprisingly, realizing
that you are not one

00:09:37.876 --> 00:09:40.950
and the same as your mom
can be pretty upsetting.

00:09:40.950 --> 00:09:44.360
A very gnarly example of this
would be that seeing a corpse

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:47.130
for the first time is a
traumatic reminder that we, too,

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:50.310
are a body made of material
stuff that is one day going

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:51.690
to die and decay.

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:54.010
In a less gnarly, but still gross example,

00:09:54.010 --> 00:09:57.420
this can also happen when we
see an open wound, or, like,

00:09:57.420 --> 00:09:58.330
fecal matter,
(gas passes)

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:00.030
as it reminds us that our own bodies

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:02.260
are full of blood and (bleep),

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:04.390
and we're basically
animals, but also human,

00:10:04.390 --> 00:10:08.000
but still animal, which
is super confusing.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:10.560
In particular, Kristeva
associates this process

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:11.970
with the maternal figure,

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:15.240
and that horrifying moment in
which we separate ourselves

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:16.180
from our mother.

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:17.220
Also important to note

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:19.210
that Kristeva's account doesn't map on

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:21.120
to the same age progression as Freud's,

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:23.800
with much of this development
taking place from birth

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:26.910
through age four, though
it repeats throughout life.

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:30.490
For Kristeva, experiencing
the abject is a necessary part

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:32.830
of the development process
on the individual's way

00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:35.170
to language and a full self-consciousness,

00:10:35.170 --> 00:10:36.500
or as Kristeva puts it,

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:39.920
abjection is a
pre-condition of narcissism,

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:42.170
with narcissism here
being less about loving

00:10:42.170 --> 00:10:43.130
to look in the mirror,

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:45.590
and more about recognizing
that there's a you

00:10:45.590 --> 00:10:48.070
to look at in the mirror
in the first place.

00:10:48.070 --> 00:10:51.070
Now "Turning Red"
definitely hints at the idea

00:10:51.070 --> 00:10:53.050
that dropping your uterine lining

00:10:53.050 --> 00:10:55.170
can provide an abject experience,

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:57.670
which can lead to
distancing from the mother,

00:10:57.670 --> 00:10:58.670
even if she's not-

00:10:58.670 --> 00:11:00.883
- The Lord visited Eve with a curse.

00:11:00.883 --> 00:11:03.400
And the curse was a curse of blood.

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:05.850
- That said, this is only hinted at,

00:11:05.850 --> 00:11:08.470
and not explicitly explored in the movie,

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:09.700
which is why we're more interested

00:11:09.700 --> 00:11:12.080
in the film's primary
metaphor for the abject,

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:14.410
which is, of course, the red panda.

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:17.770
It puts Mei at the unstable
boundary of the human

00:11:17.770 --> 00:11:18.760
and the animal.

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:21.620
She wants to just be a normal human kid,

00:11:21.620 --> 00:11:25.150
but is haunted by this wild
animal that exists within her.

00:11:25.150 --> 00:11:28.130
It's an identity crisis that
Kristeva might have warned

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:30.270
could lead to melancholia, depression,

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:32.460
and even aggressive acting out,

00:11:32.460 --> 00:11:34.950
which we see in both
Mei's initial reaction

00:11:34.950 --> 00:11:35.910
to becoming panda,

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:40.070
and her near murder of her
Nelly-idolizing classmate, Tyler.

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:43.060
So how do we deal with
these abject experiences

00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:46.370
that send our fragile
developing egos reeling?

00:11:46.370 --> 00:11:47.910
Kristeva says that both art

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:49.980
and religion can serve the purpose

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:53.690
of purifying the abject,
i.e. they give us images

00:11:53.690 --> 00:11:56.970
and narratives that make
gross stuff feel less gross.

00:11:56.970 --> 00:11:59.670
And we see something similar
happening in the film.

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:00.870
First, the mythology

00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:03.060
around Mei's family lineage is represented

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:04.550
in art throughout the temple,

00:12:04.550 --> 00:12:06.980
which shows her brave ancestor who took

00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:09.480
on the whole panda bit
to protect her family.

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:11.710
This turns the potentially aggressive

00:12:11.710 --> 00:12:13.740
and frightening animality of the panda

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:16.230
into a story about courage and bravery.

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:19.140
And, of course, the panda
can only be truly purified

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:21.180
from Mei's person via a ritual

00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:23.240
that takes place during a red moon.

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:25.470
Kristeva says that "the various means

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:29.470
of purifying the abject make
up the history of religions,

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:33.540
and end up with that catharsis
par excellence called art."

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:35.590
And in Mei's case, it's very clear

00:12:35.590 --> 00:12:37.420
that much of her family's own mythology

00:12:37.420 --> 00:12:39.960
and religious practice
serve this very purpose:

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:42.080
to purify the dangerous animal,

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:44.150
and make it part of a larger narrative.

00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:45.040
But to be clear,

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:48.700
for Kristeva, this
purifying is not necessarily

00:12:48.700 --> 00:12:49.810
a good thing.

00:12:49.810 --> 00:12:51.050
Rather, it's a process

00:12:51.050 --> 00:12:54.510
by which we avoid directly
confronting the horrifying

00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:57.410
and upsetting aspects of our own humanity.

00:12:57.410 --> 00:13:00.490
She sees art and religion
as attempting to wallpaper

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:03.380
over the breakdowns
associated with the abject.

00:13:03.380 --> 00:13:04.980
But back to our ritual.

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:08.160
Mei gets sort of teleported
up to an ancestral realm,

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:10.840
where she's led to separate
herself from her panda,

00:13:10.840 --> 00:13:12.560
but right at the crucial moment,

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:14.320
where she is about to be purified

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:17.140
of this seemingly abject
thing once and for all,

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:19.420
she remerges with it, comes back to Earth,

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:22.030
embodying her panda, and joyfully flies

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:25.490
over Toronto's rooftops on
her way to the 4*TOWN concert.

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:27.110
And yes, just like Mei's mom,

00:13:27.110 --> 00:13:29.470
we can't stop wondering
why it's called 4*TOWN

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:31.515
if there are five of them.

00:13:31.515 --> 00:13:34.130
Is one of them meant
to be the town, right?

00:13:34.130 --> 00:13:35.060
Like, how does it work?

00:13:35.060 --> 00:13:35.893
At this moment,

00:13:35.893 --> 00:13:38.100
rather than continue to repress the panda,

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:41.570
she embraces it as one
aspect of her complex self.

00:13:41.570 --> 00:13:43.950
In this way, she is
accepting the split nature

00:13:43.950 --> 00:13:47.880
of her own subjectivity, or
personhood, or, um, pandahood,

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:50.900
rather than seeking some
unifying sense of self.

00:13:50.900 --> 00:13:55.190
It's accepting rather than
purifying this abject thing.

00:13:55.190 --> 00:13:58.100
- The point isn't to
push the bad stuff away.

00:13:58.100 --> 00:14:02.570
It's to make room for it, live with it.

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:04.180
- [Michael] This is great for Mei,

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:06.450
not so great for her mother, Ming,

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:08.270
who nearly loses her damn mind,

00:14:08.270 --> 00:14:10.680
releases her own truly terrifying panda,

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:12.650
and goes on a legit rampage.

00:14:12.650 --> 00:14:16.220
The size of Ming's panda
seems like a kind of metaphor

00:14:16.220 --> 00:14:18.300
for what happens when strong feelings

00:14:18.300 --> 00:14:20.010
are repressed for decades,

00:14:20.010 --> 00:14:22.940
and the toxic effect this
can have on the psyche.

00:14:22.940 --> 00:14:24.410
And it's hinted out throughout the film

00:14:24.410 --> 00:14:27.070
that Ming has a
less-than-healthy relationship

00:14:27.070 --> 00:14:27.970
with her own mother.

00:14:27.970 --> 00:14:29.300
- Ming, it's your mother.

00:14:29.300 --> 00:14:30.133
- I'm not here.

00:14:31.100 --> 00:14:32.750
- [Michael] A relationship
that seems to be marked

00:14:32.750 --> 00:14:33.920
by the type of depression

00:14:33.920 --> 00:14:36.360
and melancholia that Kristeva associates

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:38.080
with female children's inability

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:40.150
to fully separate from their mothers.

00:14:40.150 --> 00:14:42.390
And speaking of Mei's
grandmother, eventually,

00:14:42.390 --> 00:14:45.080
all the women in her family
unleashed their own pandas,

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:47.760
showing that when they use
their passion for a purpose,

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:49.260
it's not all bad.

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:50.740
After Ming, Grandma,

00:14:50.740 --> 00:14:53.310
and the aunties all
return their pandas back

00:14:53.310 --> 00:14:54.610
to the ancestral realm,

00:14:54.610 --> 00:14:56.280
Mei reaffirms her commitment

00:14:56.280 --> 00:14:58.470
to accepting her panda and keeping it

00:14:58.470 --> 00:15:00.675
as an unrepressed part of her identity.

00:15:00.675 --> 00:15:02.503
- I'm changing, Mom.

00:15:03.871 --> 00:15:06.940
I'm finally figuring out who I am.

00:15:06.940 --> 00:15:08.530
- [Michael] She knows that in doing so,

00:15:08.530 --> 00:15:10.630
she'll be severing her
bond with her mother,

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:13.420
one that Ming has tried to
maintain throughout the film.

00:15:13.420 --> 00:15:14.890
In embracing the panda,

00:15:14.890 --> 00:15:17.820
it's as if Mei is acknowledging
the fundamental separation

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:19.760
between her mother and herself,

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:22.210
an acknowledgement that's
crucial to growing up.

00:15:22.210 --> 00:15:24.880
And it's not a stretch
to say that this theme

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:27.230
in particular is pretty autobiographical

00:15:27.230 --> 00:15:29.930
for writer and director Domee
Shi, who, in an interview,

00:15:29.930 --> 00:15:32.360
recounts the words of her mother,

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:34.610
the sentence she heard
upon each visit home

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:35.677
after moving out.

00:15:35.677 --> 00:15:38.850
"Oh, Domee, I wish I could
put you back in my stomach

00:15:38.850 --> 00:15:42.020
so I knew exactly where
you were at all times,"

00:15:42.020 --> 00:15:44.900
but instead, Mei is out of Ming's womb,

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:47.990
and finally partying at
the all-age karaoke spot.

00:15:47.990 --> 00:15:49.410
- Ready to get your karaoke on?

00:15:49.410 --> 00:15:50.243
- Totally.

00:15:50.243 --> 00:15:51.076
- By its end,

00:15:51.076 --> 00:15:52.680
the film seems to be a sort of warning

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:55.490
against the shame and subsequent
repression experienced

00:15:55.490 --> 00:15:57.300
by young people who are coming of age,

00:15:57.300 --> 00:16:00.120
along with an endorsement of
embracing and affirming some

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:02.910
of the more abject aspects
of their identities.

00:16:02.910 --> 00:16:05.220
In doing so, "Turning Red" challenges us

00:16:05.220 --> 00:16:07.800
to embrace all the weird
and embarrassing aspects

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:10.275
of ourselves, even the really gross stuff.

00:16:10.275 --> 00:16:11.108
(retches)

00:16:11.108 --> 00:16:12.100
But what do you guys think?

00:16:12.100 --> 00:16:13.960
Is "Turning Red" going to help a lot

00:16:13.960 --> 00:16:15.780
of young folks accept their desires,

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:17.370
rather than repressing them?

00:16:17.370 --> 00:16:19.760
Can we accept our animality,
and cope with the fact

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:22.270
that our bodies make
weird fluids sometimes?

00:16:22.270 --> 00:16:24.130
Or should we just shut
up and get good grades,

00:16:24.130 --> 00:16:25.400
'cause that's our only hope?

00:16:25.400 --> 00:16:27.590
Let us know what you
think in the comments.

00:16:27.590 --> 00:16:29.920
Big thanks to our patrons
for all your support,

00:16:29.920 --> 00:16:33.160
and be sure to check out
our revamped Patreon page.

00:16:33.160 --> 00:16:34.547
There's lots of cool stuff there.

00:16:34.547 --> 00:16:35.380
I think you'll love it.

00:16:35.380 --> 00:16:36.640
Obliterate that Subscribe button

00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:39.990
like it's a naughty boy band
leading your daughter into sin,

00:16:39.990 --> 00:16:42.370
and don't forget to ring
that bell, and as always,

00:16:42.370 --> 00:16:43.203
thanks for watching.

00:16:43.203 --> 00:16:44.200
Later.

00:16:44.200 --> 00:16:46.867
(upbeat music)

